{"entryType":"UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)","primaryAccession":"Q865C5","uniProtkbId":"UBIQ_CAMDR","entryAudit":{"firstPublicDate":"2004-08-31","lastAnnotationUpdateDate":"2025-02-05","lastSequenceUpdateDate":"2004-08-31","entryVersion":79,"sequenceVersion":2},"annotationScore":2.0,"organism":{"scientificName":"Camelus dromedarius","commonName":"Dromedary","synonyms":["Arabian camel"],"taxonId":9838,"lineage":["Eukaryota","Metazoa","Chordata","Craniata","Vertebrata","Euteleostomi","Mammalia","Eutheria","Laurasiatheria","Artiodactyla","Tylopoda","Camelidae","Camelus"]},"proteinExistence":"3: Inferred from homology","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"value":"Ubiquitin"}}},"comments":[{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000250"}],"value":"Ubiquitin Exists either covalently attached to another protein, or free (unanchored). When covalently bound, it is conjugated to target proteins via an isopeptide bond either as a monomer (monoubiquitin), a polymer linked via different Lys residues of the ubiquitin (polyubiquitin chains) or a linear polymer linked via the initiator Met of the ubiquitin (linear polyubiquitin chains). Polyubiquitin chains, when attached to a target protein, have different functions depending on the Lys residue of the ubiquitin that is linked: Lys-6-linked may be involved in DNA repair; Lys-11-linked is involved in ERAD (endoplasmic reticulum-associated degradation) and in cell-cycle regulation; Lys-29-linked is involved in proteotoxic stress response and cell cycle; Lys-33-linked is involved in kinase modification; Lys-48-linked is involved in protein degradation via the proteasome; Lys-63-linked is involved in endocytosis, DNA-damage responses as well as in signaling processes leading to activation of the transcription factor NF-kappa-B. Linear polymer chains formed via attachment by the initiator Met lead to cell signaling. Ubiquitin is usually conjugated to Lys residues of target proteins, however, in rare cases, conjugation to Cys or Ser residues has been observed. When polyubiquitin is free (unanchored-polyubiquitin), it also has distinct roles, such as in activation of protein kinases, and in signaling (By similarity)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"commentType":"SUBCELLULAR LOCATION","subcellularLocations":[{"location":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000250"}],"value":"Cytoplasm","id":"SL-0086"}},{"location":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000250"}],"value":"Nucleus","id":"SL-0191"}}]},{"texts":[{"value":"Ubiquitin is synthesized as a polyubiquitin precursor with exact head to tail repeats, the number of repeats differ between species. In some species there is a final amino-acid after the last repeat. Some ubiquitin genes contain a single copy of ubiquitin fused to a ribosomal protein (either eL40 or eS31)"}],"commentType":"MISCELLANEOUS"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305"}],"value":"Belongs to the ubiquitin family"}],"commentType":"SIMILARITY"}],"features":[{"type":"Chain","location":{"start":{"value":1,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":76,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Ubiquitin","featureId":"PRO_0000114793"},{"type":"Domain","location":{"start":{"value":1,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":76,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Ubiquitin-like","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000255","source":"PROSITE-ProRule","id":"PRU00214"}]},{"type":"Site","location":{"start":{"value":54,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":54,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Interacts with activating enzyme"},{"type":"Site","location":{"start":{"value":68,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":68,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Essential for function"},{"type":"Site","location":{"start":{"value":72,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":72,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Interacts with activating enzyme"},{"type":"Cross-link","location":{"start":{"value":6,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":6,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Glycyl lysine isopeptide (Lys-Gly) (in